WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.359
Rust gives developers strong tools for
building safer software.

2
00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:06.818
Ownership, borrowing, lifetimes, and the
type system reduce many classes of

3
00:00:06.898 --> 00:00:10.172
memory-safety errors that have
historically affected systems software.

4
00:00:10.252 --> 00:00:13.215
That is a meaningful security advantage,
especially for services,

5
00:00:13.295 --> 00:00:16.934
command-line tools, libraries, and backend
components that need performance and

6
00:00:17.014 --> 00:00:17.637
reliability.

7
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.992
Memory safety, however, is not the same as
complete application security.

8
00:00:21.072 --> 00:00:23.723
Rust does not automatically prevent broken
access control,

9
00:00:23.803 --> 00:00:27.078
weak authentication, insecure business
logic, sensitive data exposure,

10
00:00:27.158 --> 00:00:29.549
unsafe configuration, or poor operational
behavior.

11
00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.644
A Rust service can still trust the wrong
input, expose the wrong data,

12
00:00:32.724 --> 00:00:36.050
log secrets, accept a risky dependency, or
deploy with a dangerous default.

13
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.821
Secure Rust development uses the
language's strengths while still engineering the

14
00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:41.252
whole application carefully.

15
00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:44.191
Teams need clear boundaries for input,
explicit error behavior,

16
00:00:44.271 --> 00:00:47.078
dependency governance, secret handling,
concurrency design,

17
00:00:47.158 --> 00:00:50.537
logging discipline, testing, release
review, and operational monitoring.

18
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.164
The compiler can help with important
classes of mistakes,

19
00:00:53.244 --> 00:00:55.635
but it does not replace threat-aware
design or review.

20
00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:59.457
This course introduces the practical
habits that help teams build Rust applications

21
00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:02.916
that are safer in design, implementation,
dependency management, release,

22
00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:03.672
and operation.

23
00:01:03.752 --> 00:01:06.766
The focus is defensive and
engineering-focused: reduce ambiguity,

24
00:01:06.846 --> 00:01:10.329
keep risky boundaries small, make behavior
testable, protect sensitive data,

25
00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:13.060
and maintain visibility after the
application is deployed.
